# Zentra API Starter Bot — environment variables.
#
# Copy this to .env and fill it in. Values here are read ONCE, at startup —
# for anything that should change without a restart, see the `settings`
# table and the admin dashboard, not this file.

# ---- your bot -----------------------------------------------------------

# From @BotFather.
BOT_TOKEN=

# Comma-separated Telegram user ids. Only these accounts can use the bot's
# own admin commands — separate from the dashboard's ADMIN_PASSWORD below,
# which gates the web dashboard itself, not the bot.
ADMIN_IDS=

# ---- Zentra -----------------------------------------------------------

# From @ZentraShopBot — Menu → API Link → Create API key. This is YOUR
# key, used for every order this bot places. It is never shown to your own
# customers and never leaves this .env file.
ZENTRA_API_KEY=

# Almost never needs changing.
ZENTRA_API_BASE_URL=https://api.zentradigital.shop

# ---- your database -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# A free PostgreSQL database from Supabase works well. Create a project,
# then Project Settings → Database → Connect → choose the SESSION pooler
# (port 5432, not 6543) — see bot/db.py for why the session pooler and not
# the transaction pooler, if you are used to the opposite advice.
#
# A password containing @ # or : must be percent-encoded (@ -> %40, # -> %23).
DATABASE_URL=

# ---- payment rails -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# ALL FOUR ARE OPTIONAL. With none of these set, the bot still runs — an
# admin credits customer balances by hand from the dashboard's "Credit by
# hand" page, exactly the fallback every rail below keeps forever. See
# docs/PAYMENTS.md for what each one needs and how to get it.

# -- USDT (BEP-20) --
# Your own receiving address — a public address, never a private key or
# seed phrase. The bot only WATCHES this address for incoming transfers,
# by polling BSC_HTTP_URL every few seconds — see bot/chain/rpc.py for why
# this starter polls a single endpoint rather than running the event-driven
# multi-provider WebSocket listener ZentraShopBot's own bot uses.
BSC_PAYMENT_ADDRESS=
# One HTTP JSON-RPC endpoint for BNB Smart Chain. Free tiers exist at
# providers like dRPC, Ankr, PublicNode, or run your own node.
BSC_HTTP_URL=
# Reserved for a future event-driven upgrade — nothing reads this today.
# Leave it blank; it is not required to turn the rail on.
BSC_WSS_URL=
# USDT's own BEP-20 contract. This default is correct almost always.
USDT_CONTRACT_ADDRESS=0x55d398326f99059fF775485246999027B3197955

# Turning the rail ON also needs usdt_enabled = yes in the settings table —
# flip it on the admin dashboard's Settings page, or for now:
#   UPDATE settings SET value = 'yes' WHERE key = 'usdt_enabled';
# Both this .env config AND that setting must agree; either one missing
# means customers are never offered the top-up screen.

# -- Binance Pay --
#
# NOT A MERCHANT INTEGRATION — deliberately. Binance Pay's merchant API
# needs a business account and an approval process; this rail instead
# reads YOUR OWN personal Binance account's Pay history through a signed,
# read-only API key. A customer sends an ordinary Binance Pay transfer to
# your Binance ID (the UID below), and the bot confirms it by asking your
# own account what it received. Exactly how ZentraShopBot itself does this.
#
# Create the API key in Binance → Profile → API Management. It only ever
# needs read permission — never enable withdrawals or trading on it.
#
# Your Binance ID (a UID, not your username or email) — Binance app →
# Profile, shown near the top. This is what customers send to.
BINANCE_UID=
BINANCE_API_KEY=
BINANCE_API_SECRET=
# Almost never needs changing.
BINANCE_API_BASE=https://api.binance.com

# -- Telebirr / Bank of Abyssinia --
#
# THESE FOUR LINES ARE THE WHOLE SETUP. Receipts are verified against the
# provider automatically, through Zentra, using the ZENTRA_API_KEY above —
# there is no verifier URL to find and no second key to generate.
#
# Why it works that way: Telebirr's receipt lookup only answers Ethiopian IP
# addresses, so a bot on a VPS anywhere else cannot ask Telebirr anything.
# Zentra runs that lookup from inside Ethiopia for you.
#
# Your receiving account, per rail — this is who a customer pays, and what
# every receipt is checked against. A rail with no account set here is not
# offered at all; see bot/config.py's telebirr_configured.
TELEBIRR_NUMBER=
TELEBIRR_NAME=
ABYSSINIA_ACCOUNT=
ABYSSINIA_NAME=
#
# THE NAME IS NOT DECORATION. Telebirr redacts the middle of the receiver's
# account on a receipt ("2519****0207"), leaving four digits — which collide
# once in ten thousand. The account name is what turns those four digits
# into a real match, so a receipt with no name to check against is refused
# rather than guessed at. Write it exactly as Telebirr shows it.

# -- Running your own verification instead (optional) ----------------------
#
# Leave these blank unless you have a reason. Fill BOTH in to point at your
# own [LocalPaymentVerify](https://github.com/snackshell/localpaymentverify)
# instance rather than Zentra's — worth doing if you already have an
# Ethiopian server, want lower latency, or simply do not want the
# dependency. One without the other is ignored: a URL with no key would be
# an open endpoint, and quietly falling back to Zentra when you MEANT to
# self-host would send receipts somewhere you did not intend.
#
# Bank of Abyssinia has no Ethiopian-IP restriction, so that half can run
# anywhere in the world. Telebirr genuinely cannot.
TELEBIRR_VERIFY_URL=
TELEBIRR_VERIFY_KEY=

# -- Reading a receipt from a screenshot (optional) ------------------------
#
# Lets a customer send a photo of their receipt instead of typing the
# reference. A vision model reads ONLY the reference number off the image;
# the payment is then verified against the provider exactly as if it had
# been typed.
#
# A SCREENSHOT IS NEVER PROOF OF PAYMENT. Anything else the model claims to
# see — the amount, the receiver, "Completed" — is thrown away on purpose,
# because a picture can be edited in a minute and a provider's own record
# cannot. See bot/receiptscan.py.
#
# This is your OpenRouter key because the calls are billed per image to
# whoever makes them. Leave it blank and the feature is simply off:
# customers type the reference, which costs nothing and always works.
# Get a key at https://openrouter.ai
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# Optional. Any vision model OpenRouter offers; defaults to a cheap, fast
# one. Change it to trade cost against accuracy.
OPENROUTER_MODEL=

# ---- admin dashboard ------------------------------------------------------
#
# The dashboard is a SEPARATE Next.js app in dashboard/, with its own env
# file: dashboard/.env.example. It reads and writes the SAME database as
# the bot (that's the only thing it shares), so nothing from this file is
# needed there beyond DATABASE_URL — copy dashboard/.env.example to
# dashboard/.env.local (or set the same two variables in your deploy
# platform's project settings) and fill it in separately.
